# Operator workflow — testing and installs

## Browser / visual testing

Do not spin up browser automation, install browser tooling, or attempt visual verification on your own.

If manual or browser-based testing would help (animations, layout, hover states, etc.):

1. Stop implementation work.
2. Tell the operator what you want to verify and why.
3. Propose options (e.g. operator checks locally, shared dev server URL, screenshots) and wait for direction.

Do not install Playwright, Puppeteer, Chromium, or similar tools to work around missing browser access.

## Installing packages or tooling

Do not run `npm install`, `npx … install`, `pip install`, or add new dev dependencies without explicit operator approval.

If a dependency or global tool seems necessary:

1. Stop work.
2. Explain what you want to install, why, and any lighter alternatives.
3. Wait for the operator to approve or choose an approach before proceeding.

Routine project scripts already in `package.json` (e.g. `npm run dev`, `npm test`, `npm run build`) are fine when needed for the task — but adding new packages is not.
